From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] RescueParty: restart instead of wiping on a device whose
 recovery is another system

When system_server crashes five times in five minutes, RescueParty escalates:
it resets settings, and at its last level wipes the user's data. On PW3 Android
Test V50, where a bug of this port crashed system_server every 14 seconds, it
reached that level 44 times in 18 minutes. The data survived only because this
device has no /misc partition and no recovery to reboot into ("uncrypt failed
with status: -1", "Reboot failed (no permissions?)") - by accident, not by
design.

A wipe is the wrong answer here in any case. This Android runs from a file in
the Kindle's own storage, started from its library; restarting leaves Android
and lands in the stock system, where the same install can be repeated or an
older one put back. The data the wipe would destroy is the user's books and
accounts, and what broke is a system image that the wipe does not touch.

With ro.rescue_party.no_wipe set, the last level restarts the device instead.
The settings levels before it are unchanged: a bad persisted setting is still
worth resetting, and that costs the user nothing.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/RescueParty.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/RescueParty.java
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 import android.os.Build;
 import android.os.Environment;
 import android.os.FileUtils;
+import android.os.PowerManager;
 import android.os.RecoverySystem;
 import android.os.SystemClock;
 import android.os.SystemProperties;
@@ -56,6 +57,8 @@
     private static final String PROP_RESCUE_BOOT_COUNT = "sys.rescue_boot_count";
     private static final String PROP_RESCUE_BOOT_START = "sys.rescue_boot_start";
     private static final String PROP_VIRTUAL_DEVICE = "ro.hardware.virtual_device";
+    /** Set by a device that has no recovery to wipe with, and another system to return to. */
+    private static final String PROP_NO_WIPE = "ro.rescue_party.no_wipe";
 
     private static final int LEVEL_NONE = 0;
     private static final int LEVEL_RESET_SETTINGS_UNTRUSTED_DEFAULTS = 1;
@@ -188,7 +191,19 @@
                 resetAllSettings(context, Settings.RESET_MODE_TRUSTED_DEFAULTS);
                 break;
             case LEVEL_FACTORY_RESET:
-                RecoverySystem.rebootPromptAndWipeUserData(context, TAG);
+                if (SystemProperties.getBoolean(PROP_NO_WIPE, false)) {
+                    // A device whose recovery is another system on the same
+                    // flash: restarting leaves Android and lands there, where
+                    // the install can be repeated or rolled back. Wiping the
+                    // user's data would destroy what is still good to fix what
+                    // a new system image broke, and there is no recovery
+                    // partition to wipe with in the first place.
+                    Slog.w(TAG, "Restarting instead of wiping user data");
+                    context.getSystemService(PowerManager.class)
+                            .reboot("rescue-party-no-wipe");
+                } else {
+                    RecoverySystem.rebootPromptAndWipeUserData(context, TAG);
+                }
                 break;
         }
     }
